scenarios.ebpf_operations:
Partly expressible.
- bpftime's memcapture pass calls the kprobe/__memcapture program before each PTX line matching ^(ld|st).(const|global|local|param)?.<scalar type> a, b;. Because the optional state space sits between two mandatory dots, it matches only ld/st with an explicit const/global/local/param state space (ld.param included). Tested with python3 -I on sample lines, it misses:
  - generic ld.u32;
  - ld.shared;
  - ld.global.nc;
  - vector .v2/.v4 forms;
  - cp.async and atomics.
- The program receives a pointer to the PTX instruction text, not the effective address.
- So counting matched instructions works. Bytes per access could be parsed from the text [inferred]. Coalescing or cache-line counts need the address and a new pass argument.
- Neutrino instead keeps a per-thread counter in a probe register and saves once at kernel end.
- bpftime has no state that survives between probe calls except maps. So each access does a map read-modify-write: 1502 per thread, or 1503 shared. The mem_trace example does a non-atomic *val = *val + 1 on 1503 [inferred: concurrent lanes lose counts].
- Frequency: per memory instruction per thread.

checked.correction:
bpftime's launchlate GPU probe has no thread guard. Every thread of every vectorAdd launch runs it:
- an ARRAY lookup;
- a 1503 lookup with a non-atomic *count += 1, or a BPF_NOEXIST update;
- exactly one of the two printk sites (one host call per thread).
So host calls per launch equal the thread count, and the histogram counts threads, not launches. Concurrent lanes can also lose increments [inferred]. Only gpu_ext's launchlate (1513 FIFO) is gated to thread (0,0,0) of block (0,0,0), once per launch.

threadscheduling makes 5 host-call sites per thread per launch, not 1:
- a thread_mapping update keyed per thread;
- sm_histogram lookup plus NOEXIST insert;
- warp_histogram lookup plus NOEXIST insert.
The per-thread thread_mapping record is not aggregatable per warp (agg=False). Only the histogram sites are. All 5 are classed defer.

added.claim:
CUDA's own device printf already defers output:
- a fixed-size circular buffer, 1 MB by default, set via cudaLimitPrintfFifoSize;
- older output is overwritten on overflow;
- output is flushed only at synchronization or blocking calls, never while the kernel runs.
This is a vendor precedent for buffering printk-class output on the GPU, and it has the same hang/overflow caveat.
